            <ArticleTitle>Coronary artery pseudothrombus: angiographic filling defects caused by competitive collateral flow.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>196-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Two cases are described of intraluminal coronary artery filling defects resembling thrombus caused by nonopacified collateral blood flow mixing with injected contrast just distal to a severe coronary artery stenosis. The term pseudothrombus is ascribed to this appearance to emphasize this differential diagnosis of intraluminal filling defects.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
